diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — pc1 (195.11.14.2) unable to reach pc2 (200.1.1.2) — does not match the live state of the network. Direct probing shows the connection is fully operational: a 4-packet ICMP test from pc1 to pc2 completes with 0% packet loss and sub-millisecond round-trip times.

Every layer of the data and control plane is healthy. The inter-router link (router1:eth0 193.10.11.1 ↔ router2:eth0 193.10.11.2) is UP on both ends with zero errors and zero drops, directly refuting the operations team's theory that "the cable between the two buildings went down." The eBGP session between AS 1 (router1) and AS 2 (router2) has been continuously established for over two minutes, with each peer correctly advertising its local prefix and installing the remote one: router1 holds B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via router2, and router2 holds B>* 195.11.14.0/24 via router1. Both routers carry clean configurations with no route-maps, prefix-lists, or access-lists that could filter traffic. The host interfaces on pc1 and pc2 are both UP with correct addresses and no packet drops.

Root cause: There is no fault — the network is healthy and end-to-end connectivity between pc1 and pc2 is intact.

Recommended action: No corrective fix is needed on the network side. The helpdesk ticket should be closed as a false alarm. The user or application on pc1 should be re-tested; the earlier connectivity loss was likely transient (e.g., a brief BGP convergence event at lab startup) and has since self-resolved.
